1️⃣ My first call (one-shot)

csharpusing Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
using OllamaSharp;

// OllamaApiClient implements IChatClient from Microsoft.Extensions.AI
IChatClient chat = new OllamaApiClient(
    new Uri("http://localhost:11434"),
    "phi3:mini");

var response = await chat.GetResponseAsync(
    "Explain a NullReferenceException in one sentence.");

Console.WriteLine(response.Text);

I make sure Ollama is running (ollama serve or the app), then dotnet run.
A working generative-AI app in ~8 lines.


2️⃣ Streaming (what I actually want)

The one-shot call waits for the entire response. For anything user-facing, I stream tokens as they generate — this surfaces the autoregressive loop from Day 6 (one token at a time).

csharpawait foreach (var update in chat.GetStreamingResponseAsync(
    "Explain what a memory leak is to a C# developer."))
{
    Console.Write(update.Text);   // prints each chunk as it arrives
}


3️⃣ Day 6 concepts → real parameters

System prompts and temperature were theory in Day 6. Here I turn them into code.

csharpusing Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
using OllamaSharp;

IChatClient chat = new OllamaApiClient(new Uri("http://localhost:11434"), "phi3:mini");

var messages = new List<ChatMessage>
{
    // System prompt — sets role + constraints (Day 6, technique #1)
    new(ChatRole.System,
        "You are a log analysis assistant for .NET developers. " +
        "Explain errors in plain English. Be concise."),

    new(ChatRole.User,
        "Log: NullReferenceException in PaymentService line 42")
};

var options = new ChatOptions
{
    Temperature = 0.1f   // low = focused/deterministic — right for log analysis
};

await foreach (var update in chat.GetStreamingResponseAsync(messages, options))
    Console.Write(update.Text);

Multi-turn note to self: each call is stateless. To make it conversational, I append the model's reply back into the messages list before the next user message — I carry the history (Day 6 context-window idea).


✅ My key takeaways:
Gen AI in C# = an HTTP call. Ollama runs a REST API on localhost:11434; my app is just a client.
Two packages, two jobs. Microsoft.Extensions.AI = the interface; OllamaSharp = the Ollama driver.
IChatClient is provider-agnostic. The same interface targets Ollama today and Azure OpenAI / GPT-4o tomorrow — one-line swap.
I stream for anything real. GetStreamingResponseAsync shows the autoregressive token loop and feels responsive.
Day 6 theory is now parameters. System prompt = role/constraints · temperature = focused (0.1) vs creative (0.9).
The model is stateless. Conversation history is my responsibility — I keep it in a List<ChatMessage>.
I avoid the deprecated package. Microsoft.Extensions.AI.Ollama is dead; I use OllamaSharp.

Day 8 one-liner
Generative AI in C# is just an HTTP call to a local server —
IChatClient is the function, the system prompt is the config, and temperature is the dial.
